WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:06.871
This slide explains drift detection in a governed AWS landing zone.

2
00:00:06.871 --> 00:00:10.260
First, look at AWS Control Tower on the left.

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:18.534
It establishes an approved governance baseline, including supported controls and resources managed as part of the landing zone.

4
00:00:18.534 --> 00:00:21.613
Follow the arrow to drift detection in the center.

5
00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:28.830
Drift means that an account, governed resource, or managed configuration no longer matches the expected state.

6
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:36.227
The detection step tells operators that something has changed, but it does not mean every type of drift is fixed automatically.

7
00:00:36.227 --> 00:00:38.430
The next box is remediation.

8
00:00:38.554 --> 00:00:50.527
Depending on the drift type, an administrator may need to repair configuration, reset an affected control, re-enroll or update an account, or use an explicitly configured automation.

9
00:00:50.527 --> 00:00:56.661
The baseline box beneath the process represents the reference state against which changes are assessed.

10
00:00:56.661 --> 00:01:05.192
The teaching point is to identify exactly what drifted, determine why, choose a supported corrective action and then verify the result.

11
00:01:05.192 --> 00:01:16.184
Keep AWS Config rule noncompliance and CloudFormation stack drift distinct from Control Tower-specific landing-zone drift; they can be related but are not interchangeable.

12
00:01:16.306 --> 00:01:21.875
The architectural objective is reliable governance, not blind automatic rollback.

13
00:01:28.008 --> 00:01:34.866
This slide shows why AWS CloudFormation StackSets is useful in a large multi-account environment.

14
00:01:34.866 --> 00:01:37.533
Begin with the StackSets service on the left.

15
00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:43.023
A platform team defines one approved CloudFormation template and a set of parameters.

16
00:01:43.023 --> 00:01:48.860
Follow the connections toward AWS Organizations and the three target accounts in the center.

17
00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:57.469
Each account can receive a stack instance in the specified AWS Regions, illustrated here by the Region labels below the account names.

18
00:01:57.576 --> 00:02:09.936
This is how teams deploy consistent baseline resources, such as approved security configurations, monitoring or common IAM infrastructure, without manually copying templates to every account.

19
00:02:09.936 --> 00:02:19.910
A StackSet operation can create, update or delete the associated stack instances through a centralized process, subject to permissions and target selection.

20
00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:38.789
Two permission models matter for SAP-C03: service-managed permissions integrate with Organizations and support OU targeting and eligible automatic deployments to newly added accounts; self-managed permissions require you to configure the necessary administration and execution roles.

21
00:02:38.904 --> 00:02:47.514
Plan rollout concurrency, failure tolerance, drift detection and rollback behavior rather than assuming every target updates successfully.

22
00:02:47.514 --> 00:02:55.942
Remember that StackSets provides consistent distribution, while service-specific authorization and configuration remain necessary.

23
00:03:02.092 --> 00:03:07.647
This slide introduces three complementary AWS cost-governance capabilities.

24
00:03:07.647 --> 00:03:15.122
On the left, separate production, staging and development accounts contribute usage and charges to consolidated billing.

25
00:03:15.122 --> 00:03:17.390
Now follow the three arrows on the right.

26
00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:26.939
AWS Budgets tracks configured cost or usage thresholds and can send notifications when actual or forecast spending crosses an alert level.

27
00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:34.297
Cost Categories provides a business-oriented view of spending by rules, such as product, department or environment.

28
00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:48.004
Cost-allocation tags identify resources or workloads so selected costs can be attributed to owners or applications; appropriate tags must be activated for billing and may not apply to every charge.

29
00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:51.548
The example at the bottom makes the interaction concrete.

30
00:03:51.548 --> 00:04:03.584
A production budget reaches its chosen threshold; an email or SNS alert notifies the FinOps or platform team, which investigates the underlying usage and takes a considered action.

31
00:04:03.584 --> 00:04:10.014
A budget warning is not an automatic EC2 resize or a guarantee that future charges stop.

32
00:04:10.132 --> 00:04:21.138
For architecture reviews, build useful tagging and grouping conventions early, define accountable budget owners, and verify the visibility required across the organization.

33
00:04:21.138 --> 00:04:26.692
Together these tools support financial control without replacing engineering decisions.
